.page init 8 the first process, the shrine maiden

# SYNOPSIS
*init*

# DESCRIPTION
`init` is process 1. When the kernel has mounted the filesystem and unpacked the initrd it starts `/sbin/init`, which opens the shrine for the day: it works out who is at the door, starts that resident's session, and when the session ends sees who comes next. It also adopts and collects every orphaned process.

Before anything else `init` reads Keine's records: the files of `/Hakurei/etc` that a HUMANVILLAGE volume keeps in `/HumanVillage/.system/etc` (passwords, accounts, the host name, the time zone, the network and update settings). Each one is checked and put over the default from the boot image; a damaged one is left out with a line in the kernel log. A USB stick whose volume is mounted only after the first session started is read before the next session. With `nohumanvillage` on the command line the records are left alone. See etckeep(8).

# THE DOOR
By default the door is open for `reimu`: her desktop starts at once (or her login shell, on a machine without a display or with `text` on the command line). Logging out of a desktop always returns to the door, so switching user is *Shut down... -> Log out*. When a resident leaves, every process they left behind is sent home.

With `login` on the command line (or `autologin=none`) the door asks who is there: on a machine with a display the login screen (`hakurei-greeter`, running as root in its own window server), otherwise the `login` prompt on the console. The login screen has a **Text console** button that opens the `login` prompt once and returns to the login screen when that shell ends. A login screen that keeps dying (three times in a row) gives way to the text prompt for good. A desktop that dies, or is shot down with Ctrl+Alt+Backspace, does not hand out a shell for free either: it opens the text prompt, then the login screen again.

The environment `init` hands to a session has `HOME`, `USER`, `LOGNAME`, `SHELL`, `PATH=/bin:/sbin:/usr/bin`, `HOSTNAME`, `TERM=xterm` and `TZ` when one is set; the login profile adds the rest (see profile(5)).

# KERNEL COMMAND LINE
The words `init` looks at (they are set in `cmdline.txt` on the boot volume):

@ text, nogui
    No desktop: a text session on the console.
@ login
    Ask who is at the door instead of letting `reimu` in.
@ autologin=NAME
    Open NAME's session (default `reimu`); `none` asks at the door. `user=root` is the old spelling for `autologin=root`.
@ tz=ZONE
    The time zone, in the POSIX form (see timezone(5)).
@ rtc=local
    The clock chip keeps local time (a PC that also runs Windows does that): correct the system clock by the zone's offset.
@ utest
    Do not start a session: run utest(1) and power the machine off.

If a program started by `init` dies within a second it is not started again at once, so a broken installation cannot spin the machine.

# SEE ALSO
login(1), gapsh(1), profile(5), timezone(5), sudoers(5), gensokyo(7), accounts(7), utest(1)
